                                ;============================================================
                                ; SEARCH  (/ ? n N)
                                ;
                                ; vi's search on WordMaster's matcher.  What it is NOT: vim searches with a
                                ; REGULAR EXPRESSION and this searches for the literal string typed.  An 8080
                                ; has no room for a regex engine; COMMANDS.md records the deviation.  (WM's own
                                ; search wildcards -- ^A any, ^S separator, ^O not -- still reach WCMATCH from
                                ; here, because MATCHF/MATCHB compare through it, but they are WordMaster's
                                ; syntax, not vim's, and nothing advertises them.)
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SRCH, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - '/' starts one character PAST the cursor and lands ON the match's first
                                ;     character; '?' finds the last match that ends before the cursor.
                                ;   - 'wrapscan' is on: a search that runs off one end of the file starts
                                ;     again at the other, so '/' on the file's ONLY match finds it again.
                                ;   - 'n' repeats the last search's DIRECTION and 'N' reverses it, so 'n'
                                ;     after a '?' keeps going backward.
                                ;   - An empty pattern ('/' then <CR>) repeats the last pattern.
                                ;   - A count repeats the search: '2/a' is the second match, '15n' fifteen
                                ;     n's.  Not found leaves the cursor exactly where it was, at any count.
                                ;   - The window is placed as 'G' places it -- least scroll when the match is
                                ;     near, centred when it is far -- which is why this ends in GPLACE.
                                ;
                                ; This replaces a byte-at-a-time search that ran through CHARAT over logical
                                ; offsets: it could only see the resident window (so a match further into a
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-113


                                ; paged file did not exist), had no '?' or 'N', and failed silently.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SUBSTITUTE  (:s  :%s  :N,Ms  and the 'g' flag)
                                ;
                                ; vi's substitute on WordMaster's matcher, so it finds the same LITERAL string
                                ; the search does -- not a regular expression.  COMMANDS.md records that
                                ; deviation for '/' and it is the same one here, which is why nothing below
                                ; honours '&', '\1' or '~'.  The pattern goes into the SEARCH's own buffer
                                ; because vim's ':s' also sets the last search pattern, so 'n' afterwards
                                ; repeats it.
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SUBST, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - ':s' changes the FIRST match on the cursor's line, ':s...g' every match,
                                ;     and the scan goes on PAST the replacement, so a replacement that
                                ;     contains the pattern is not substituted again.
                                ;   - the cursor lands on the first non-blank of the LAST line changed, placed
                                ;     exactly as 'G' places a line -- which is what GZONE before and GPLACE
                                ;     after are for, and it is measured: ':12800,12800s' leaves the last line
                                ;     on row 22 (top 012778) and ':100,105s' leaves line 105 on row 11.
                                ;   - a pattern that is nowhere in the range changes nothing and moves nothing.
                                ;
                                ; A whole substitute is ONE change for 'u': the second UNDBEG, at the first
                                ; match, is what makes the undo's cursor the first match (where vim leaves it)
                                ; rather than the line the ':' was typed on.  A substitute of more than about a
                                ; dozen lines overruns the undo region, which is accepted -- but it SAYS so,
                                ; both when it happens and when 'u' is then pressed.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;EXADDR -- the optional line range before an ex command: '%' (the whole file),
                                ;'N', or 'N,M'.  SRNGF = 0 when there is none, and SRNG2 = 0FFFFH is the last
                                ;line, whatever it turns out to be.  HL comes back on the command letter.
                                ;
                                ; It does NOT consume the blanks after itself: VC_EX's SKIPB does that, so
                                ; ':2,4 s/a/Z/' reads as ':2,4s/a/Z/' the way ex has always taken it.  The one
                                ; place a blank is still refused is INSIDE the range, around the comma
                                ; (':2, 4s'), which ex accepts and this does not -- the comma is read here off
                                ; DE mid-number and skipping there costs more than the position is worth.
  24F9'                         EXADDR:
  24F9'   AF                    	XRA	A
  24FA'   32 2B3B'              	STA	SRNGF
  24FD'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  24FE'   FE 25                 	CPI	'%'
  2500'   CA 2529'              	JZ	EXA_AL
  2503'   EB                    	XCHG			; DE walks the line, HL takes the number
  2504'   CD 253E'              	CALL	EXNUM
  2507'   DA 2527'              	JC	EXA_NO
  250A'   22 2B3C'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  250D'   22 2B3E'              	SHLD	SRNG2		; 'N' alone is the single line 'N,N'
  2510'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  2511'   FE 2C                 	CPI	','
  2513'   C2 2520'              	JNZ	EXA_YS
  2516'   13                    	INX	D
  2517'   CD 253E'              	CALL	EXNUM
  251A'   DA 2527'              	JC	EXA_NO		; ',' with no number: not a range at all
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-114


  251D'   22 2B3E'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  2520'                         EXA_YS:
  2520'   EB                    	XCHG
  2521'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2523'   32 2B3B'              	STA	SRNGF
  2526'   C9                    	RET
  2527'                         EXA_NO:
  2527'   EB                    	XCHG
  2528'   C9                    	RET
  2529'                         EXA_AL:
  2529'   23                    	INX	H
  252A'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  252B'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  252E'   22 2B3C'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  2531'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  2534'   22 2B3E'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  2537'   E1                    	POP	H
  2538'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  253A'   32 2B3B'              	STA	SRNGF
  253D'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EXNUM -- a decimal number at DE -> HL, DE left past it.  CY when there is no
                                ;digit there at all.
  253E'                         EXNUM:
  253E'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  253F'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  2541'   D8                    	RC
  2542'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  2544'   3F                    	CMC
  2545'   D8                    	RC
  2546'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2549'                         EXN_LP:
  2549'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  254A'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  254C'   DA 2566'              	JC	EXN_DN
  254F'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  2551'   D2 2566'              	JNC	EXN_DN
  2554'   13                    	INX	D
  2555'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2556'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  2557'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; BC = the digit
  2559'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  255A'   54                    	MOV	D,H
  255B'   5D                    	MOV	E,L
  255C'   29                    	DAD	H		; *2
  255D'   29                    	DAD	H		; *4
  255E'   19                    	DAD	D		; *5
  255F'   29                    	DAD	H		; *10
  2560'   D1                    	POP	D
  2561'   09                    	DAD	B
  2562'   C1                    	POP	B
  2563'   C3 2549'              	JMP	EXN_LP
  2566'                         EXN_DN:
  2566'   B7                    	ORA	A		; CY clear: there was a number
  2567'   C9                    	RET
                                
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-115


                                ;EX_S -- ':s': the delimiter, the pattern, the replacement, an optional 'g'.
  2568'                         EX_S:
  2568'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2569'   B7                    	ORA	A
  256A'   CA 25AC'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s' alone -- repeating the last one is not
                                				;   built (COMMANDS.md)
  256D'   32 2B40'              	STA	SDELIM
  2570'   23                    	INX	H
  2571'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2574'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; the pattern's capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  2577'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  2578'   CD 25B2'              	CALL	SCOPY
  257B'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  257C'   B7                    	ORA	A
  257D'   CA 25AC'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s//x/' -- an empty pattern matches nothing
  2580'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		; WM's RDSTR layout: the length below the string
  2583'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2585'   32 2B08'              	STA	SHAVE		; 'n' repeats it, as after a '/'
  2588'   11 2B52'              	LXI	D,SREPL
  258B'   0E 24                 	MVI	C,SREPMX
  258D'   CD 25B2'              	CALL	SCOPY
  2590'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2591'   32 2B51'              	STA	SREPLN
  2594'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2595'   32 2B41'              	STA	SGFLAG
  2598'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2599'   FE 67                 	CPI	'g'
  259B'   C2 25A4'              	JNZ	EX_SG
  259E'   23                    	INX	H
  259F'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  25A1'   32 2B41'              	STA	SGFLAG
  25A4'                         EX_SG:
  25A4'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  25A5'   B7                    	ORA	A
  25A6'   C2 2333'              	JNZ	EX_TRL		; 'Trailing chars: ' -- vim's, shortened
  25A9'   C3 25D2'              	JMP	SUBGO
  25AC'                         SUBNOP:
  25AC'   21 23FB'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  25AF'   C3 2341'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;SCOPY -- the text at HL up to SDELIM (or the line's end) into [DE], C its
                                ;capacity: B = how many were taken, HL past the delimiter, and [DE] is left
                                ;0-terminated as WM's RDSTR leaves a string.
  25B2'                         SCOPY:
  25B2'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  25B4'                         SCP_LP:
  25B4'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  25B5'   B9                    	CMP	C
  25B6'   D2 25CF'              	JNC	SCP_DN		; full: what is left stays on the line, and so
                                				;   comes back as trailing characters
  25B9'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  25BA'   B7                    	ORA	A
  25BB'   CA 25CF'              	JZ	SCP_DN		; the line ended: no closing delimiter wanted
  25BE'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  25BF'   21 2B40'              	LXI	H,SDELIM
  25C2'   BE                    	CMP	M
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-116


  25C3'   E1                    	POP	H
  25C4'   CA 25CE'              	JZ	SCP_SK
  25C7'   12                    	STAX	D
  25C8'   13                    	INX	D
  25C9'   23                    	INX	H
  25CA'   04                    	INR	B
  25CB'   C3 25B4'              	JMP	SCP_LP
  25CE'                         SCP_SK:
  25CE'   23                    	INX	H
  25CF'                         SCP_DN:
  25CF'   AF                    	XRA	A
  25D0'   12                    	STAX	D
  25D1'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBGO -- run the substitute.  GZONE comes first, before anything moves: the
                                ;landing is placed as 'G' places a line, and GPLACE measures it against the
                                ;screen's top line as it was when the command was typed.
  25D2'                         SUBGO:
  25D2'   CD 0FBB'              	CALL	GZONE
  25D5'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  25D8'   22 2B4F'              	SHLD	SLDLTS		; the walk moves the cursor; a substitute that
  25DB'   21 2B4C'              	LXI	H,SOFFS		;   finds nothing puts both back, as the
  25DE'   CD 0429'              	CALL	FPSTO		;   search's SN_BAD does
  25E1'   AF                    	XRA	A
  25E2'   32 2B42'              	STA	SANY
  25E5'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  25E8'   22 2B44'              	SHLD	SBACK
  25EB'   3A 2B3B'              	LDA	SRNGF
  25EE'   B7                    	ORA	A
  25EF'   CA 2601'              	JZ	SUB_1L
                                	;--- ':N,Ms' / ':%s': to the range's first line, then walk it ---
  25F2'   CD 1EC1'              	CALL	EXRNG		; SLINES = the lines in the range
  25F5'   DA 267D'              	JC	SUBBAD
  25F8'   2A 2B3C'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  25FB'   CD 0F29'              	CALL	GOLN
  25FE'   C3 2607'              	JMP	SUBLP
  2601'                         SUB_1L:
  2601'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2604'   22 2B46'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2607'                         SUBLP:
  2607'   CD 2683'              	CALL	SUBLIN		; A = 1 when this line changed
  260A'   B7                    	ORA	A
  260B'   CA 2619'              	JZ	SUB_NX
  260E'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2610'   32 2B42'              	STA	SANY
  2613'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2616'   22 2B44'              	SHLD	SBACK		; the cursor is ON the last line changed
  2619'                         SUB_NX:
  2619'   2A 2B46'              	LHLD	SLINES
  261C'   2B                    	DCX	H
  261D'   22 2B46'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2620'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2621'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2622'   CA 2640'              	JZ	SUBDON		; the range is spent
  2625'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2628'   AF                    	XRA	A
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-117


  2629'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; the next line, paging with it
  262C'   3A 2B42'              	LDA	SANY
  262F'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2630'   CA 263A'              	JZ	SUB_AE		; nothing changed yet: nothing to count from
  2633'   2A 2B44'              	LHLD	SBACK
  2636'   23                    	INX	H
  2637'   22 2B44'              	SHLD	SBACK		; every step taken after the last change has
                                				;   to be walked back at the end -- including
                                				;   this one, when it lands past the last line
  263A'                         SUB_AE:
  263A'   CD 0A63'              	CALL	ATEOF
  263D'   D2 2607'              	JNC	SUBLP		; ... unless that ran off the end of the file
  2640'                         SUBDON:
  2640'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the walk left the count and direction set
  2643'   3A 2B42'              	LDA	SANY
  2646'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2647'   CA 2665'              	JZ	SUBNFD
  264A'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  264C'   32 2A88'              	STA	MODF		; the text changed, so ':w' has work to do.  NOT
                                				;   MARKMOD: that also sets DOTCHG, and '.' does
                                				;   not repeat a substitute in vim either (that
                                				;   is '&', which is not built)
  264F'   2A 2B44'              	LHLD	SBACK
  2652'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2653'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2654'   CA 265F'              	JZ	SUB_PL
  2657'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH
  2659'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; back to the last line changed
  265C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  265F'                         SUB_PL:
  265F'   CD 0FD3'              	CALL	GPLACE		; the landing: first non-blank, placed as 'G'
  2662'   C3 07C1'              	JMP	SETFUL		; GPLACE asks for R_MOVE when the target is
                                				;   which rewrote the line under the cursor.
                                				; And that repaint is why a substitute too big
                                				;   for the undo does NOT say so here: EXMSG
                                				;   draws the bottom row there and then, and
                                				;   the full paint this needs takes the status
                                				;   row back (SCRN.MAC says so itself).  'u'
                                				;   carries the message instead -- UNDWHY = 2,
                                				;   at the moment the reader asks for the undo
                                				;   and can still act on the answer.
  2665'                         SUBNFD:
  2665'   21 2B4C'              	LXI	H,SOFFS
  2668'   CD 03D0'              	CALL	BACKTO
  266B'   2A 2B4F'              	LHLD	SLDLTS
  266E'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing changed: the row the cursor was on
  2671'   21 23CB'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  2674'   CD 2341'              	CALL	EXERR
  2677'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  267A'   C3 233C'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
  267D'                         SUBBAD:
  267D'   21 23B9'              	LXI	H,SNOT
  2680'   C3 2336'              	JMP	EXERT
                                
                                ;SUBLIN -- the substitute on the cursor's line: the first match, or every match
                                ;when 'g' was given.  A = 1 when the line changed.  The cursor is left at the
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-118


                                ;line's start either way, so the walk to the next line always starts from one.
                                ;Every offset is re-derived from the cursor after each change, never held
                                ;across one: an insert can page (':%s/0/ZZ/' grows a 100 K file by 12800
                                ;bytes) and paging shifts every logical offset in the window.
  2683'                         SUBLIN:
  2683'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2684'   32 2B43'              	STA	SLCHG
  2687'   CD 2702'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  268A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET0		; the line has to be RESIDENT before CEND can
  268D'                         SUBL_R:				;   find its end: the pager keeps the CURSOR in
  268D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET		;   the window, not the whole line it sits on,
  2690'   DA 2698'              	JC	SUBL_L		;   and EOLNL only ever scans resident text --
  2693'   FE 0A                 	CPI	LF		;   so a line straddling the resident end came
  2695'   C2 268D'              	JNZ	SUBL_R		;   out short and went unsubstituted (line 179
                                				;   of the 66 K indented file, which starts at
                                				;   byte 2047).  RGET pages through NEEDIN, as
                                				;   every other line reader here does.
  2698'                         SUBL_L:
                                	;--- the scan window: the cursor to the line's content end ---
  2698'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  269B'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  269C'   CD 1575'              	CALL	CEND
  269F'   D1                    	POP	D
  26A0'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = the bytes left on the line
  26A3'   23                    	INX	H		; FWDCNT's own arithmetic, inline: the span it
  26A4'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		;   would measure, plus one, less the pattern.
  26A7'   7C                    	MOV	A,H		;   (FWDCNT needs a real pointer pair and this
  26A8'   B7                    	ORA	A		;   span is a line, not a pair.)
  26A9'   FA 26FB'              	JM	SUBL_D		; what is left is shorter than the pattern
  26AC'   44                    	MOV	B,H
  26AD'   4D                    	MOV	C,L		; BC = the start positions a match can take
  26AE'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  26B1'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; with the gap at the cursor, the line runs
  26B4'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF		;   forward from GAPEND
  26B7'   DA 26FB'              	JC	SUBL_D
  26BA'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends past the match; back to its start
  26BD'   EB                    	XCHG
  26BE'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND
  26C1'   EB                    	XCHG
  26C2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = how far into the scan the match is
  26C5'   EB                    	XCHG
  26C6'   D5                    	PUSH	D		; CUROFF does NOT keep DE (it reads TXTBEG into
  26C7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   it), so the delta goes on the stack
  26CA'   D1                    	POP	D
  26CB'   19                    	DAD	D		; ... and so the match's logical offset
  26CC'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
                                	;--- the first change of the whole substitute re-arms the undo, so 'u'
                                	;    lands on the first match, which is where vim leaves it ---
  26CF'   3A 2B42'              	LDA	SANY
  26D2'   B7                    	ORA	A
  26D3'   C2 26E0'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  26D6'   3A 2B43'              	LDA	SLCHG
  26D9'   B7                    	ORA	A
  26DA'   C2 26E0'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  26DD'   CD 0000*              	CALL	UNDHER		; the cursor snapshot only: UNDBEG would re-run
                                				;   UNDGRW mid-command, and growing the region
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-119


                                				;   where a primitive can see it corrupts the
                                				;   gap ('COPY ERR')
  26E0'                         SUBL_C:
  26E0'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCHBF
  26E3'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  26E4'   26 00                 	MVI	H,0
  26E6'   CD 0000*              	CALL	DELFWD		; the match goes
  26E9'   11 2B51'              	LXI	D,SREPLN	; ... and the replacement takes its place, the
  26EC'   CD 0000*              	CALL	INSSTR		;   way WM splices one: the length byte with
                                				;   its string above it, 'u' capture included
  26EF'                         SUBL_I:
  26EF'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  26F1'   32 2B43'              	STA	SLCHG
  26F4'   3A 2B41'              	LDA	SGFLAG
  26F7'   B7                    	ORA	A
  26F8'   C2 2698'              	JNZ	SUBL_L		; 'g': on from PAST the replacement
  26FB'                         SUBL_D:
  26FB'   CD 2702'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  26FE'   3A 2B43'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2701'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBBOL -- the cursor to the start of the line it is on.
  2702'                         SUBBOL:
  2702'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2705'   CD 0624'              	CALL	BOL
  2708'   C3 0000*              	JMP	GOTO
                                
                                ;VC_SRCH -- '/' and '?': read the pattern, then search.  C is the key, which
                                ; is both the prompt character and the direction.
  270B'                         VC_SRCH:
  270B'   CD 07A0'              	CALL	SETMOV
  270E'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  270F'   CD 06CB'              	CALL	GETCNT
  2712'   22 2B31'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2715'   C1                    	POP	B
  2716'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2717'   FE 2F                 	CPI	'/'
  2719'   3E 00                 	MVI	A,0
  271B'   CA 2720'              	JZ	VS_DIR
  271E'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH		; '?' -- backward
  2720'                         VS_DIR:
  2720'   32 2B06'              	STA	SDIR
  2723'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST	; the line is read straight into the pattern
  2726'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; its capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  2729'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  272A'   CD 1C6A'              	CALL	RDLINE
  272D'   B7                    	ORA	A
  272E'   C0                    	RNZ			; cancelled: nothing happens at all
  272F'   3A 2ADD'              	LDA	RLLEN
  2732'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2733'   CA 2748'              	JZ	VS_OLD		; an empty line repeats the last pattern --
                                				;   RDLINE stored nothing, so it is still there
  2736'   5F                    	MOV	E,A
  2737'   16 00                 	MVI	D,0
  2739'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  273C'   19                    	DAD	D
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-120


  273D'   36 00                 	MVI	M,0		; 0-terminated, and the length below it:
  273F'   7B                    	MOV	A,E
  2740'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		;   WM's RDSTR layout, built by hand
  2743'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2745'   32 2B08'              	STA	SHAVE
  2748'                         VS_OLD:
  2748'   3A 2B08'              	LDA	SHAVE
  274B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  274C'   CA 2758'              	JZ	VS_NOP		; '/' <CR> with nothing ever searched for
  274F'   3A 2B06'              	LDA	SDIR
  2752'   32 2B07'              	STA	SDIRE
  2755'   C3 2781'              	JMP	SRCHN
  2758'                         VS_NOP:
  2758'   21 23FB'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  275B'   C3 2341'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;VC_N / VCB_N -- 'n' repeats the last search, 'N' reverses its direction.
  275E'                         VC_N:
  275E'   CD 07A0'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2761'   3A 2B06'              	LDA	SDIR
  2764'   C3 276E'              	JMP	VN_GO
  2767'                         VCB_N:
  2767'   CD 07A0'              	CALL	SETMOV
  276A'   3A 2B06'              	LDA	SDIR
  276D'   2F                    	CMA			; the other way: 0 <-> FFH
  276E'                         VN_GO:
  276E'   32 2B07'              	STA	SDIRE
  2771'   CD 06CB'              	CALL	GETCNT
  2774'   22 2B31'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2777'   3A 2B08'              	LDA	SHAVE
  277A'   B7                    	ORA	A
  277B'   CA 2758'              	JZ	VS_NOP
  277E'   C3 2781'              	JMP	SRCHN
                                
                                ;SRCHN -- the count, one whole-file search each.  Not found at ANY repeat
                                ; leaves the cursor where the command started, as vim does, and says so; the
                                ; message wants the screen left alone, which is what EXERR's R_MOVE is for.
  2781'                         SRCHN:
  2781'   CD 0FBB'              	CALL	GZONE		; the screen's top line, before the cursor moves
  2784'   CD 06F4'              	CALL	BRKON		; where it started (the sweep moves the cursor
                                				;   with PUTCUR, which does not maintain LNDLT;
                                				;   GOTO does), and an ESC may stop it
  2787'                         SN_LP:
  2787'   CD 27BC'              	CALL	SRCHGO
  278A'   DA 27A7'              	JC	SN_BAD
  278D'   2A 2B31'              	LHLD	SCNT
  2790'   2B                    	DCX	H
  2791'   22 2B31'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2794'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2795'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2796'   C2 2787'              	JNZ	SN_LP
  2799'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the sweep left CMDCNT wide open (and DIRFLG
                                				;   backward): WM clears both per command
  279C'   CD 0731'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  279F'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  27A1'   32 2B13'              	STA	GPKEEP		; land on the match, not the first non-blank
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-121


  27A4'   C3 0FD3'              	JMP	GPLACE		; (which sets LNDLT itself)
  27A7'                         SN_BAD:
  27A7'   CD 0731'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  27AA'   DA 0749'              	JC	BRKBAK		; stopped, which is not the same as not found
  27AD'   CD 073C'              	CALL	RSTPL		; nothing moved: the row the cursor was on
  27B0'   21 23CB'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  27B3'   CD 2341'              	CALL	EXERR
  27B6'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  27B9'   C3 233C'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
                                
                                ;SRCHGO -- one search of the whole file, wrapping once: from the cursor to the
                                ; end in SDIRE's direction, then from the other end back to it.  CY = the
                                ; pattern is nowhere in the file (and the cursor is left at an end of it --
                                ; SRCHN is what puts it back).
  27BC'                         SRCHGO:
  27BC'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  27BE'   32 2B3A'              	STA	SSKIP		; this pass starts one past the cursor
  27C1'   CD 27D7'              	CALL	SRCHW
  27C4'   D0                    	RNC
  27C5'   CD 0735'              	CALL	BRKQ
  27C8'   D8                    	RC			; stopped: no second pass
  27C9'   3A 2B07'              	LDA	SDIRE
  27CC'   2F                    	CMA
  27CD'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLMAX		; to the end the wrapped pass starts from
  27D0'   AF                    	XRA	A
  27D1'   32 2B3A'              	STA	SSKIP		; ... but the wrapped pass starts AT it, or a
                                				;   match on the file's very first character
                                				;   would be stepped over and never found
  27D4'   C3 27D7'              	JMP	SRCHW
                                
                                ;SRCHW -- WM FINDA (WM 1943): the file from the cursor on, one resident
                                ; window at a time.  A miss moves the cursor to that window's edge and pages
                                ; one step (SCRLST), so the search runs off the window and through the file;
                                ; CY when the file runs out.  PAGESTP only pages while the count is unspent,
                                ; so the sweep opens CMDCNT wide, as MVLMAX does.  WM's FINDA then puts the
                                ; cursor exactly at the file's end (WM 1949); nothing here needs that -- the
                                ; caller either wraps to the other end or restores the cursor outright.
  27D7'                         SRCHW:
  27D7'   3A 2B07'              	LDA	SDIRE
  27DA'   32 0000*              	STA	DIRFLG
  27DD'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  27E0'   22 2A85'              	SHLD	CMDCNT
  27E3'   AF                    	XRA	A
  27E4'   32 0000*              	STA	AUXCNT
  27E7'                         SW_LP:
  27E7'   CD 0735'              	CALL	BRKQ		; an ESC spent the count, here or on the way to
  27EA'   D8                    	RC			;   the wrapped pass: SCRLST no longer pages
  27EB'   CD 27F7'              	CALL	SRCH1
  27EE'   D0                    	RNC
  27EF'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SCRLST
  27F2'   D2 27E7'              	JNC	SW_LP
  27F5'   37                    	STC
  27F6'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SRCH1 -- WM FINDB (WM 1984) without its RDSTR: one search inside the
                                ; resident window, DIRFLG picking the direction.  CY = not in this window; on
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-122


                                ; a hit the cursor is ON the match's first character.
  27F7'                         SRCH1:
  27F7'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  27FA'   3A 0000*              	LDA	DIRFLG
  27FD'   B7                    	ORA	A
  27FE'   FA 2822'              	JM	SR1_BK
  2801'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,GAPEND
  2804'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2807'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; WM loads the pointer BEFORE testing the
  280A'   D8                    	RC			;   carry: a miss still has to leave HL a
                                				;   real text address, because SCRLST puts
                                				;   the cursor there on the way to paging
  280B'   3A 2B3A'              	LDA	SSKIP
  280E'   B7                    	ORA	A
  280F'   CA 2818'              	JZ	SR1_FW
  2812'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2813'   B1                    	ORA	C
  2814'   37                    	STC
  2815'   C8                    	RZ			; no room for a match even before the skip
  2816'   0B                    	DCX	B		; vim's '/' starts one character PAST the
  2817'   23                    	INX	H		;   cursor, so the character under it can
                                				;   never be the one that matches
  2818'                         SR1_FW:
  2818'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF
  281B'   D8                    	RC
  281C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends PAST the match; vi lands on it
  281F'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
  2822'                         SR1_BK:
  2822'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TXTBEG
  2825'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2828'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPBEG		; (as above: the pointer, then the carry)
  282B'   D8                    	RC
  282C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHB already lands on the first character
  282F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHB
  2832'   D8                    	RC
  2833'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DISPATCH TABLES -- the messages first, then CMDTAB and ACTTAB.
                                ;============================================================
                                ;============================================================
                                ; MESSAGES -- the bottom row is vim's message line: blank unless something has
                                ; been said on it.  A message is built by successive calls -- EXPRM first, which
                                ; clears the row, then the pieces straight to the terminal.  There is no
                                ; concatenation buffer and none is wanted: the pieces are a name, a flag and a
                                ; number, and each knows how to write itself.
                                ;
                                ; Every message is drawn AFTER the frame (MSGPST, from the main loop), because
                                ; a full repaint blanks the bottom row -- so a message written during the
                                ; command would be wiped by the repaint that same key causes.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;MSGNAM -- open the row and write the file name at HL (a 12-byte FCB field:
                                ; drive, 8 name, 3 type) in quotes, the way vim opens a message: "TEST.TXT".
                                ; A blank name field is vim's "[No Name]".  vim writes a path relative to the
                                ; current directory -- a file in it bare, "../x/VI.MAC" otherwise -- and the
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-123


                                ; logged drive is CP/M's current directory, so the drive is written only when
                                ; it is another one: "B:TEST.TXT".
  2836'                         MSGNAM:
  2836'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2837'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2839'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXPRM		; clears the row, writes the opening quote
  283C'   E1                    	POP	H
  283D'   E5                    	PUSH	H		; keep the base: the type field is 9 past it
  283E'   23                    	INX	H
  283F'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2840'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2842'   C2 284F'              	JNZ	MN_HAS
  2845'   21 2469'              	LXI	H,SNONM
  2848'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  284B'   E1                    	POP	H
  284C'   C3 2899'              	JMP	MN_END
  284F'                         MN_HAS:
  284F'   0E 19                 	MVI	C,BF_CURDSK
  2851'   CD 0005               	CALL	BDOS		; A = the logged drive, A: = 0
  2854'   E1                    	POP	H
  2855'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2856'   3C                    	INR	A		; the FCB's drive byte is explicit, A: = 1
  2857'   BE                    	CMP	M
  2858'   CA 2866'              	JZ	MN_NAM
  285B'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  285C'   C6 40                 	ADI	'A'-1
  285E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2861'   3E 3A                 	MVI	A,':'
  2863'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2866'                         MN_NAM:
  2866'   E1                    	POP	H
  2867'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2868'   23                    	INX	H
  2869'   0E 08                 	MVI	C,8
  286B'                         MN_NM:
  286B'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  286C'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  286E'   CA 2879'              	JZ	MN_EXT
  2871'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2874'   23                    	INX	H
  2875'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2876'   C2 286B'              	JNZ	MN_NM
  2879'                         MN_EXT:
  2879'   E1                    	POP	H
  287A'   11 0009               	LXI	D,9
  287D'   19                    	DAD	D		; the type field
  287E'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  287F'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2881'   CA 2899'              	JZ	MN_END
  2884'   3E 2E                 	MVI	A,'.'
  2886'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2889'   0E 03                 	MVI	C,3
  288B'                         MN_XL:
  288B'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  288C'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  288E'   CA 2899'              	JZ	MN_END
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-124


  2891'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2894'   23                    	INX	H
  2895'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2896'   C2 288B'              	JNZ	MN_XL
  2899'                         MN_END:
  2899'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  289B'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTCH
                                
                                ;MSGFLG -- the flags after the name, in vim's order and with vim's spelling:
                                ; [Modified][readonly][New], run together with ONE space before the first of
                                ; them (measured from vim 9.1).  A = 0 leaves [Modified] out, which is the load
                                ; message, where nothing has been changed yet.
  289E'                         MSGFLG:
  289E'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; B: a flag has been written (the space goes once)
  28A0'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28A1'   CA 28B1'              	JZ	MF_RO
  28A4'   3A 2A88'              	LDA	MODF
  28A7'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28A8'   CA 28B1'              	JZ	MF_RO
  28AB'   21 2473'              	LXI	H,SMODM
  28AE'   CD 28C6'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  28B1'                         MF_RO:
  28B1'   3A 2AF3'              	LDA	ROFLAG
  28B4'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28B5'   CA 28BE'              	JZ	MF_NEW
  28B8'   21 247E'              	LXI	H,SROM
  28BB'   CD 28C6'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  28BE'                         MF_NEW:
  28BE'   3A 2AF1'              	LDA	MSGNEW
  28C1'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28C2'   C8                    	RZ
  28C3'   21 2489'              	LXI	H,SNEWM
  28C6'                         MF_PUT:
  28C6'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  28C7'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28C8'   C2 28D4'              	JNZ	MF_STR
  28CB'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  28CD'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  28CE'   3E 20                 	MVI	A,' '
  28D0'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  28D3'   E1                    	POP	H
  28D4'                         MF_STR:
  28D4'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;EMITW -- HL as unsigned decimal, no leading zeros (0 still prints one digit).
                                ; Repeated subtraction of each power of ten, which is all an 8080 has.  B
                                ; carries 'a digit has been printed' so the leading zeros are skipped.
  28D7'                         EMITW:
  28D7'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  28D8'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  28D9'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  28DB'   11 2710               	LXI	D,10000
  28DE'   CD 28FC'              	CALL	EWDIG
  28E1'   11 03E8               	LXI	D,1000
  28E4'   CD 28FC'              	CALL	EWDIG
  28E7'   11 0064               	LXI	D,100
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-125


  28EA'   CD 28FC'              	CALL	EWDIG
  28ED'   11 000A               	LXI	D,10
  28F0'   CD 28FC'              	CALL	EWDIG
  28F3'   7D                    	MOV	A,L		; the units digit is always printed
  28F4'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  28F6'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  28F9'   D1                    	POP	D
  28FA'   C1                    	POP	B
  28FB'   C9                    	RET
  28FC'                         EWDIG:
  28FC'   0E 00                 	MVI	C,0
  28FE'                         EW_LP:
  28FE'   7D                    	MOV	A,L
  28FF'   93                    	SUB	E
  2900'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2901'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2902'   9A                    	SBB	D
  2903'   67                    	MOV	H,A
  2904'   DA 290B'              	JC	EW_DN
  2907'   0C                    	INR	C
  2908'   C3 28FE'              	JMP	EW_LP
  290B'                         EW_DN:
  290B'   19                    	DAD	D		; one subtraction too many: give it back
  290C'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  290D'   B7                    	ORA	A
  290E'   C2 2914'              	JNZ	EW_PR
  2911'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2912'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2913'   C8                    	RZ			; a leading zero: write nothing
  2914'                         EW_PR:
  2914'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2915'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2917'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  291A'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  291C'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;ISEMPT -- Z iff the buffer holds nothing at all: no resident text, and nothing
                                ; spilled behind the window either.  vim's '--No lines in buffer--', shortened.
  291D'                         ISEMPT:
  291D'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	OUTPOS
  2920'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2921'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2922'   C0                    	RNZ
  2923'   CD 0000*              	CALL	LOGLEN
  2926'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2927'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2928'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;MSGCTG -- '^G': the file, its flags, and where the cursor is.  DEVIATION: vim
                                ; also gives the file's line total and a percentage of it; no line total is kept
                                ; anywhere here, and counting one means a sweep of the whole file out to EOF and
                                ; a page back -- which would cost the undo region (PGTOPM) as well as the wait.
  2929'                         MSGCTG:
  2929'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  292C'   CD 2836'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  292F'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1		; '^G' is where vim shows [Modified]
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-126


  2931'   CD 289E'              	CALL	MSGFLG
  2934'   CD 291D'              	CALL	ISEMPT
  2937'   C2 2940'              	JNZ	MG_POS
  293A'   21 249C'              	LXI	H,SNOLIN
  293D'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
  2940'                         MG_POS:
  2940'   21 248F'              	LXI	H,SLINE
  2943'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2946'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CNTLF		; the line feeds before the cursor ...
  2949'   23                    	INX	H		;   ... so the line it is on
  294A'   CD 28D7'              	CALL	EMITW
  294D'   21 2496'              	LXI	H,SCOLM
  2950'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2953'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CBOL		; the byte column within the line, 0-based
  2956'   23                    	INX	H
  2957'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2958'   CD 28D7'              	CALL	EMITW
  295B'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CURDCL		; the column a TAB expands that byte to
  295E'   23                    	INX	H
  295F'   D1                    	POP	D
  2960'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; the same -> vim writes one number, not two
  2963'   C8                    	RZ
  2964'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2965'   3E 2D                 	MVI	A,'-'
  2967'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  296A'   E1                    	POP	H
  296B'   C3 28D7'              	JMP	EMITW
                                
                                ;FILMSG -- the load message: the file and its flags.  vim adds the line and
                                ; byte totals ('3L, 30B'); the line total is the sweep MSGCTG's note describes.
                                ; A buffer with no file name gets no message at all, as in vim.
  296E'                         FILMSG:
  296E'   3A 0001*              	LDA	FNAME+1
  2971'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2973'   C8                    	RZ
  2974'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2977'   CD 2836'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  297A'   AF                    	XRA	A		; nothing has been changed yet
  297B'   C3 289E'              	JMP	MSGFLG
                                
                                ;WRMSG -- what ':w' says.  vim counts what it wrote ('"TEST.TXT" 3L, 29B
                                ; written'); the counts are the same sweep, so this says only that it happened.
                                ; The name is TFCB's, so ':w OTHER.TXT' names the file it really wrote.
  297E'                         WRMSG:
  297E'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TFCB
  2981'   CD 2836'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2984'   21 24AE'              	LXI	H,SWRIT
  2987'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;MSGPST -- draw the message this key asked for, once the frame is painted, and
                                ; put the cursor back.  The main loop calls it after SCDRAW and MODEMS.
  298A'                         MSGPST:
  298A'   3A 2AF0'              	LDA	MSGWHT
  298D'   B7                    	ORA	A
  298E'   C8                    	RZ
  298F'   47                    	MOV	B,A
